DA-004 — Scene 06 — The Chair / The Lie — Draft v1.0

A little after one, Eli came back to the Rider Room and stopped with the door still open behind him.

The camera had been charging on the dresser for most of an hour. Before going downstairs he had stopped the recorder, left the fan off, and let Martin sleep. He had looked once at the two chairs by the window before leaving because he had spent too much of the night learning what happened when he did not look at things first.

They were not where he remembered them.

The round table was still between them, but no longer centered. The chair Martin had used earlier had been pulled away from the table and turned toward the rain-dark window. The other had been pushed closer to the curtain, its front legs no longer parallel with the edge of the rug.

Eli did not move farther into the room.

The bathroom door stood open. The bedspread rose and fell once with Martin’s breathing. Rain tapped the glass in light bursts, and somewhere beyond the wall a pipe gave a single dull tick.

Eli looked at the camera. The charging light was still on.

The last wide shot would tell him whether he remembered the chairs correctly. He could play it back. He could put the old image beside the room now and make the change look clean.

He did not touch the camera.

He crossed to the bed and put a hand on Martin’s shoulder.

“Dad.”

Martin made a sound in his throat and turned away.

“Dad, wake up.”

Martin opened his eyes. For a second he looked past Eli without understanding where he was. Then his face tightened with recognition.

“What happened?”

“Did you move the chairs?”

Martin blinked at him.

“The chairs by the window.”

Martin pushed himself up on one elbow and looked over.

“Yeah.”

The answer came so quickly that Eli almost missed it.

“You did?”

“I woke up. Couldn’t get comfortable.” Martin rubbed his face with both hands. “I sat over there for a few minutes. Pulled one around. Bumped the other out of the way. Then I went back to bed.”

Eli turned toward the window again.

That was all.

No unexplained chair movement he had to solve. No chair dragging itself across carpet while the room sat empty. No reason to rewind the camera except to confirm a movement Martin had just admitted making.

Martin watched him wake the rest of the way.

“You thought they moved?”

Eli picked up the camera but did not unplug it.

“I thought we had something.”

Martin sat upright and looked toward the window. “I moved them.”

“You could’ve told me.”

“You were downstairs.”

Eli set the camera back down harder than he meant to. The charger plug shifted in the wall but stayed seated.

“Why did you go straight to the walls?”

Martin’s eyes moved once toward the bathroom.

“There,” Eli said. “That.”

“What?”

“You heard those knocks and you were out of that chair before I even had the camera pointed right. Then you dragged me through half the floor looking at vents and doors and service spaces. You knew why it bothered you.”

“I knew why it bothered me.” Martin’s voice was rough from sleep. “I didn’t know what made it.”

“Then tell me why.”

Martin looked down at the carpet.

Eli felt his pulse in his hands. The anger came faster than the excitement had when he first saw the chairs. “You do this every time.”

Martin looked up.

“You did it when I was a kid.”

Martin’s expression changed before he said anything.

Eli kept going. “You told me that story like it happened. I believed you because you were my dad. Then years later I asked where it came from and you told me you made it up.”

“I know.”

“You told me it happened. Then years later you got to tell me it didn’t.”

Martin put his feet on the floor.

The rain had weakened while Eli was downstairs. Without the recorder running and with both of them still, the sound in the room narrowed to water at the window, the faint electrical hum of the charger, and a distant elevator motor rising and fading somewhere in the building.

“I shouldn’t have done that,” Martin said.

“And now every time I think something happened, you stand there waiting to tell me the part I missed.”

Martin leaned forward, forearms on his knees. “Because I already did this to you once.”

Eli stared at him.

Martin did not look away this time.

“That’s why you came,” Eli said.

“It’s part of why.”

“Not because you thought we might find something.”

“I came because you asked me.”

“That isn’t the same thing.”

“No.”

Eli looked toward the wall Martin had tested first after the knocks. He could still see the sequence of the last hour as clearly as the furniture in front of him: Martin’s palm against painted plaster, his fingers on the vent grille, the locked staff door, the way his shoulders had dropped when an ordinary guest-room latch finally gave him a sound he could place.

“You were scared before we left the room,” Eli said.

Martin said nothing.

“Not after the source hunt. Before it.”

Still nothing.

Eli moved closer to the bed. “What did you recognize?”

Martin’s jaw tightened.

“Dad.”

“The knocking.”

“I know it was the knocking.”

“The pattern.”

Eli waited.

Martin pressed both palms together between his knees and stared at them. When he spoke again, his voice was quieter.

“One. Then two.”

Eli stopped breathing for a second.

He heard the old story as memory, not words: the part he had carried longest, the small pause that made the second pair feel deliberate. He had known for years that the story itself was false. That knowledge had become broad enough to cover everything in it. He had never gone back and separated which pieces Martin had borrowed, which he had changed, which he had simply invented.

“What about it?” Eli asked.

Martin looked at him.

“I made that part up.”

Eli did not answer.

“Nobody told me that rhythm,” Martin said. “I didn’t read it somewhere. It wasn’t a family story. I made up the knocks.”

Eli shook his head once.

“The whole story was made up.”

“I knew the story was fake.” Eli pointed toward the wall. “I didn’t know that was yours.”

“It was.”

“Completely?”

“Yes.”

“No old version. No story somebody told you.”

“No.”

“You just decided: one, then two.”

Martin’s mouth tightened. “Yes.”

Eli turned away from him.

The camera remained on the dresser between them and the window. Its battery icon showed full now. Beside it, the recorder lay dark where Eli had stopped it before leaving. Neither device had been running while the chairs changed position. Neither had been running while Martin slept, woke, sat by the window, and went back to bed.

Eli looked at the wall again.

“That’s why you got up.”

“Yes.”

“You recognized it before I did.”

“Yes.”

“And you didn’t tell me.”

Martin stood. “I wanted another source before I said it out loud.”

“Why?”

Martin took a step toward the window, then stopped beside the displaced chair.

“Because if I found a door or a pipe or somebody in the hall, then I could tell myself I’d put three ordinary sounds together after I heard them.”

Eli looked at him.

“And if you didn’t?”

Martin’s eyes settled on the chair back under his hand.

“I didn’t know what came after that.”

Martin stopped there. He did not offer a pipe, a door, a vent, or another guest as the next possibility.

Eli looked toward the door.

“No one here knows that story.”

Martin shook his head.

“I never told the guide.”

“I know.”

“You didn’t tell anybody.”

“No.”

The charger hummed faintly against the wall.

Neither of them spoke.

A pipe ticked somewhere beyond the bathroom and did not repeat. Rain brushed the window. Farther down the corridor, a door closed with the softened thump they had already tested.

Martin kept one hand on the chair.

Eli watched the wall.

One knock sounded from somewhere near the far side of the room.

Both men turned.

Nothing followed immediately.

Eli did not reach for the camera. Martin did not move toward the wall.

The pause lengthened until Eli felt himself start to doubt its shape, to wonder whether he had already waited too long for the rest of it.

Two knocks followed from somewhere closer to the bathroom.

Not loud.

Not sharp enough to place.

Two distinct impacts with a small space between them.

Martin’s hand left the chair.

Eli looked at him.

Martin was already looking back.

Neither of them moved.

